# NOVI(1)

## NAME

novi - full-screen text editor

## SYNOPSIS

| **novi** [ file ]

## DESCRIPTION

*Novi* edits *file* on the screen: the text is displayed, the cursor is moved over it with the
arrow keys, and what is typed appears where the cursor is. If *file* does not exist it is
created when the buffer is written; with no argument the buffer starts empty and **^O** asks
for a name.

The commands are control characters, and the two lines at the foot of the screen list the
common ones. The line above them shows messages and asks questions. The line at the top shows
the file name, whether the buffer has been changed, and whether typing inserts (**[INS]**) or
replaces (**[OVR]**).

Cursor motion:

arrow keys
: one character or one line

**^B  ^F**
: left, right

**^P  ^N**
: up, down

**^A  ^E**
: start, end of line

Home, End
: the same

**^Y  ^V**
: one screenful up, down

PgUp, PgDn
: the same

Changing the text:

**^D**
: delete the character under the cursor; Del does the same

**^H**
: delete the character before it; the erase key does the same

Ins
: switch between inserting and replacing

**^K**
: cut from the cursor to the end of the line. Struck again with nothing in between, it adds the
  next line to what was already cut instead of replacing it

**^U**
: insert everything **^K** has cut

**^R**
: insert a file at the cursor

Everything else:

**^G**
: a page describing these keys

**^O**
: write the buffer out. The current name is offered and may be edited

**^W**
: search forward for a string, wrapping round the end of the buffer

**^C**
: report the line, the column and the character position

**^X**
: leave. A changed buffer is asked about first

When *novi* asks a question, printable characters answer it, the erase key rubs one out, a
newline accepts and **^C** or ESC cancels.

Tabs are shown as spaces to the next eighth column. Other control characters are shown as
**?**. Text is treated as UTF-8 throughout: a Cyrillic letter is two bytes and one column, and
may be typed into the buffer, into a file name and into a search string alike.

## THE TERMINAL

*Novi* writes ANSI/VT100 escape sequences and reads them back from the arrow and function keys.
It consults no terminal database: termcap(5) is not read and **$TERM** is not looked at. The
console this system boots on is a Consul-254 typewriter, which is a suitable terminal only
because the simulator's line is *raw8* and passes the escapes through to whatever is at the far
end.

There is no window-size call in this kernel, so the screen is taken to be 24 lines of 80
columns unless **$LINES** and **$COLUMNS** say otherwise.

While *novi* runs, the terminal is in RAW mode with echo off.

## FILES

/tmp/noviL\*
: the text before the cursor

/tmp/noviR\*
: the text after it, stored in reverse

/tmp/noviC\*
: what **^K** has cut

All three are unlinked as soon as they are opened, so they disappear when *novi* does, however
it goes. The document is never held in memory; the two halves together take at most twice the
size of the file being edited.

## DIAGNOSTICS

*Novi* refuses a file name longer than 255 characters, and says so if the terminal or the
scratch files cannot be set up. Everything else is reported on the message line and the editor
carries on.

## AUTHOR

Dave W Plummer. This page and the port to this machine are not his.

## BUGS

**^O** writes the file in place with creat(2), so a write that fails part of the way through
leaves the file truncated. There is no rename(2) on this system to make the replacement atomic;
ed(1)'s **w** has the same exposure.

A *novi* killed by a signal it cannot catch leaves the terminal in RAW mode with echo off.
Stty(1) is the way back.

A lone ESC is not acted on until another key is struck, there being no way to time out a read.

The interactive part of *novi* has no automated test. See README.md beside the source.

## SEE ALSO

ed(1), stty(1)
